# NOTES
# 1. STRUCTURAL ANACHRONISM CONFIRMED: "Creusot-Loire" is WRONG as RPV manufacturer for Chooz B-1/2 and Civaux-1/2 (N4 palier, commissioned 1996-2002).
#    Creusot-Loire was formed in 1970 (merger of CAFL + Forges et Ateliers du Creusot) and liquidated Dec 12 1984 (fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creusot-Loire).
#    Its forge/nuclear assets were absorbed via Framatome's nationalization. N4-palier RPVs, forged in the late 1980s/early 1990s at the earliest,
#    could not have been made by a company that had ceased to exist a decade prior. Corrected manufacturer: Framatome.
# 2. PRE-1970 ANACHRONISM: Chooz-A (commissioned 1967) and Brennilis/EL-4 (built 1962-66) both predate Creusot-Loire's 1970 formation entirely.
#    Chooz-A's RPV is directly sourced to "Société des Forges et Ateliers du Creusot" (the pre-merger predecessor) - confirms the anachronism pattern
#    extends earlier than the sample audit's original P4/N4-era flag suggested.
# 3. UNRESOLVED BOUNDARY RISK (flagged, not resolved - recommend follow-up sourcing): P4-palier units commissioned 1987-1992 (Cattenom, Belleville) and
#    later CP2 units (Chinon B-3/4, commissioned ~1987-88) were commissioned close to or after Creusot-Loire's Dec-1984 liquidation. Given typical
#    multi-year RPV forging lead times, Cattenom-3 (1991) and Cattenom-4 (1992) in particular are at meaningfully higher risk of the same
#    "Creusot-Loire" anachronism as the N4 units, but no source was found confirming exact forging/order dates, so these were left UNVERIFIABLE
#    rather than called WRONG on inference alone. Worth a targeted follow-up (EDF/Framatome technical archives, INIS) before bulk-correcting.
# 4. Chooz-A's architect_engineer is WRONG: the plant was built and operated through SENA (Société d'énergie nucléaire franco-belge des Ardennes),
#    a Franco-Belgian joint venture created 1960 between EDF and Belgian utilities - not EDF acting alone as AE. This is a real exception to the
#    "EDF is always AE" fleet-wide pattern, arising because Chooz-A was a binational project.
# 5. Brennilis (EL-4) turbine_supplier is WRONG as "None": sourced confirmation the plant had a 75 MWe turbine-alternator connected to the grid in
#    July 1967 and generated ~6.32 TWh over its lifetime. The heuristic apparently assumed shutdown/experimental reactors had no turbine - false for
#    EL-4, which was a power-generating prototype, not a pure research reactor. Exact turbine manufacturer was not sourced (corrected value: Unknown).
# 6. UNGG/gas-graphite units (Bugey-1, Chinon A EDF1/2/3): "None" for rpv_manufacturer could not be confirmed or refuted with available sources.
#    These reactors used steel spherical containment (Chinon EDF1: 55m diameter steel sphere, per fr.wikipedia) or prestressed-concrete pressure
#    vessels rather than a standard forged PWR-style RPV supplied by a commercial vendor - so "None" may be a defensible DB convention for
#    "no comparable commercial RPV market participant," but this was not independently confirmed and should not be read as "CONFIRMED."
# 7. Turbine supplier for the standard PWR fleet (Alstom/Alsthom/GEC-Alsthom lineage) was directly sourced for Bugey CP0, Chinon B, Chooz B, and
#    Civaux, and only generalized (not per-plant sourced) for Belleville, Blayais, Cattenom, Cruas, and Dampierre. Note also a naming anachronism
#    risk not flagged as WRONG here: the corporate name "Alstom" (with one 's') postdates 1998; contemporaneous 1970s-90s documents would say
#    "Alsthom" or "GEC Alsthom." Treated as the same continuing entity/lineage rather than a substantive misattribution.
# 8. WebSearch quota was exhausted mid-session (shared session budget, not batch-specific); all research after that point relied on WebFetch against
#    French and English Wikipedia only. IAEA PRIS/INIS and EDF/Framatome primary sources were not directly consulted for this batch - a likely next
#    step for resolving the boundary-risk RPV cases in note 3.
